          "topic": "Define neoplasia.",
          "generatedNotes": "**Definition of Neoplasia**\n\nNeoplasia is literally translated as *new growth*. In contemporary medicine, a neoplasm is defined as a *genetic disorder* of cellular growth. It is initiated by *mutations*—most frequently acquired but occasionally inherited—that occur within a single cell.\n\n**Key Pathophysiologic Characteristics**\n\n- *Clonality*: The entire neoplasm is comprised of the *clonal progeny* of the original mutated cell.\n- *Autonomy*: Neoplastic cells gain a survival advantage, leading to *excessive proliferation* that functions independently of standard physiologic growth signals and regulatory controls.\n- *Study of Tumors*: The field of *oncology* (derived from the Greek *oncos*) is dedicated to the study of these growths. While the term *tumor* historically referred to inflammatory swelling, it is now synonymous with neoplasm.\n\n**Structural Components of a Neoplasm**\n\nEvery tumor is organized into two distinct, interdependent compartments:\n\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Component</th>\n      <th>Composition</th>\n      <th>Function</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>*Parenchyma*</td>\n      <td>Transformed neoplastic cells.</td>\n      <td>Determines the biological behavior and provides the basis for tumor classification/naming.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>*Reactive Stroma*</td>\n      <td>Connective tissue, blood vessels, and cells of the innate/adaptive immune system.</td>\n      <td>Provides the necessary framework, blood supply, and support for the growth and dissemination of the parenchyma.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Stromal Variations and Consistency**\n\nThe physical \"feel\" or consistency of a tumor is often dictated by the relationship between the parenchyma and the stroma:\n- *Desmoplasia*: A process where parenchymal cells trigger the formation of an abundant, collagen-rich stroma.\n- *Scirrhous*: Neoplasms that are \"stony hard\" due to extensive *desmoplasia* (e.g., certain breast carcinomas).\n- *Fleshy*: Neoplasms with minimal connective tissue support, resulting in a soft texture.\n\n**Pathogenesis Summary**\n\nAcquired/Inherited Mutation in a single cell &rarr; Altered gene function &rarr; *Clonal expansion* &rarr; Proliferation independent of host controls &rarr; Formation of *neoplasm* (Parenchyma + Stroma)."
