          "topic": "Describe the nomenclature of tumors.",
          "generatedNotes": "**Nomenclature of Neoplasia**\n\n**General Principles**\n- *Neoplasm* refers to a \"new growth\" resulting from genetic mutations in a single cell and its *clonal progeny*.\n- All tumors possess two components: the *parenchyma* (neoplastic cells) and the *reactive stroma* (connective tissue, blood vessels, and immune cells).\n- *Desmoplasia* describes the formation of abundant collagenous stroma stimulated by parenchymal cells; such tumors are often stony hard or *scirrhous*.\n\n**Benign Tumors**\n- **Mesenchymal Tumors**: Generally named by attaching the suffix *-oma* to the cell of origin (e.g., *fibroma* from fibroblasts, *chondroma* from cartilage).\n- **Epithelial Tumors**: Classification is based on cell of origin, microscopic patterns, or macroscopic architecture.\n    - *Adenoma*: Derived from glandular tissue, regardless of whether it forms glands.\n    - *Papilloma*: Produces fingerlike or warty projections from epithelial surfaces.\n    - *Cystadenoma*: Forms large cystic masses, common in the ovary.\n    - *Polyp*: A grossly visible projection above a mucosal surface (e.g., colonic lumen).\n\n**Malignant Tumors**\n- **Sarcoma**: Malignant tumors arising from solid *mesenchymal* tissues (e.g., *fibrosarcoma*, *leiomyosarcoma*).\n- **Carcinoma**: Malignant neoplasms of *epithelial* cell origin.\n    - *Squamous cell carcinoma*: Cells resemble stratified squamous epithelium.\n    - *Adenocarcinoma*: Neoplastic epithelial cells grow in a glandular pattern.\n- **Hematopoietic/Lymphoid**: Termed *leukemias* (blood-forming cells) or *lymphomas* (lymphocytes/precursors).\n\n**Mixed and Specialized Tumors**\n- **Mixed Tumors**: Derived from a single germ layer but show more than one line of differentiation (e.g., *pleomorphic adenoma* of the salivary gland contains epithelial and myxoid/cartilaginous elements).\n- **Teratoma**: Originates from *totipotential germ cells* (ovary/testis) and contains tissues from more than one germ cell layer (e.g., *dermoid cyst* of the ovary).\n- **Hamartoma**: A disorganized mass composed of cells indigenous to the involved site; considered benign neoplasms due to clonal chromosomal aberrations.\n- **Choristoma**: A heterotopic (misplaced) rest of normally organized tissue (e.g., a nodule of pancreatic tissue in the stomach wall).\n\n**Tumor Nomenclature Summary**\n\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Tissue of Origin</th>\n      <th>Benign</th>\n      <th>Malignant</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Fibrous Tissue</td>\n      <td>*Fibroma*</td>\n      <td>*Fibrosarcoma*</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Cartilage</td>\n      <td>*Chondroma*</td>\n      <td>*Chondrosarcoma*</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Smooth Muscle</td>\n      <td>*Leiomyoma*</td>\n      <td>*Leiomyosarcoma*</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Skeletal Muscle</td>\n      <td>*Rhabdomyoma*</td>\n      <td>*Rhabdomyosarcoma*</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Blood Vessels</td>\n      <td>*Hemangioma*</td>\n      <td>*Angiosarcoma*</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Squamous Epithelium</td>\n      <td>*Squamous cell papilloma*</td>\n      <td>*Squamous cell carcinoma*</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Glandular Epithelium</td>\n      <td>*Adenoma*</td>\n      <td>*Adenocarcinoma*</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>Melanocytes</td>\n      <td>*Nevus*</td>\n      <td>*Melanoma*</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Malignant Exceptions to the \"-oma\" Rule**\nSeveral tumors use the *-oma* suffix but are always *malignant*:\n- *Melanoma* (skin)\n- *Lymphoma* (lymphoid tissue)\n- *Mesothelioma* (mesothelium)\n- *Seminoma* (testicular germ cells)\n- *Hepatocellular carcinoma* (sometimes called hepatoma)\n\n**Pathways of Differentiation**\n\nTotipotential germ cells &rarr; Ectoderm, Mesoderm, and Endoderm &rarr; *Teratoma*\n\nSingle neoplastic clone &rarr; Epithelial and Mesenchymal lines &rarr; *Mixed Tumor / Pleomorphic Adenoma*"
