          "topic": "Explain the epidemiology of cancer.",
          "generatedNotes": "**Epidemiology of Cancer**\n\n**Global and US Impact**\n- *Mortality*: Cancer is the second most common cause of death in the United States, surpassed only by cardiovascular disease.\n- *Trends*: Mortality rates in the US began a downward trend in the late 20th century and continue to decline in the 21st century.\n- *Projections*: Global cancer deaths are expected to reach approximately 13.2 million annually by 2030 due to population growth and aging.\n\n**Cancer Incidence and Mortality by Sex**\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Category</th>\n      <th>Men (Most Common)</th>\n      <th>Women (Most Common)</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Incidence**</td>\n      <td>Prostate, Lung, Colorectal</td>\n      <td>Breast, Lung, Colorectal</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Mortality**</td>\n      <td>Lung, Prostate, Colorectal</td>\n      <td>Lung, Breast, Colorectal</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Environmental and Lifestyle Risk Factors**\n- *Infectious Agents*: Responsible for ~15% of global cancers. The burden is three times higher in developing nations (e.g., *HPV* for cervical cancer, *H. pylori* for gastric cancer).\n- *Smoking*: The primary environmental cause of death; specifically linked to 90% of lung cancers and various others (pancreas, bladder, mouth).\n- *Alcohol Consumption*: Increases risk of oropharyngeal, laryngeal, and esophageal carcinomas. Synergistic with tobacco.\n- *Diet and Obesity*: Diets high in fat and low in fiber are linked to colorectal cancer. Obesity is associated with ~14% of cancer deaths in men and ~20% in women.\n- *Reproductive History*: Prolonged, cumulative exposure to *unopposed estrogen* increases the risk for breast and endometrial cancers.\n\n**Age-Related Patterns**\n- *Adults*: Frequency increases significantly after age 55, likely due to the *accumulation of somatic mutations* and declining immune competence.\n- *Children*: Cancer is the second leading cause of death in those under 15 (after accidents). \n- *Pediatric Cancer Types*: Predominantly include *leukemias*, *CNS tumors*, and \"small round blue cell tumors\" (e.g., *Wilms tumor*, *neuroblastoma*).\n\n**Acquired Predisposing Conditions**\n- **Chronic Inflammation**: Triggers compensatory cellular proliferation and *reactive oxygen species* (ROS) that damage DNA.\n- **Precursor Lesions**: Localized morphologic changes indicating a \"field\" at risk for malignancy.\n- **Immunodeficiency**: Specifically predisposes to *virus-induced* cancers, such as lymphomas and certain carcinomas.\n\n**Common Precursor Lesions and Associations**\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Precursor Lesion</th>\n      <th>Associated Malignancy</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>*Barrett Esophagus*</td>\n      <td>Esophageal Adenocarcinoma</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>*Endometrial Hyperplasia*</td>\n      <td>Endometrial Carcinoma</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>*Colonic Villous Adenoma*</td>\n      <td>Colorectal Carcinoma</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>*Bronchial Squamous Metaplasia*</td>\n      <td>Squamous Cell Lung Carcinoma</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Genetic Predisposition**\n- Approximately 5% of cancers are primarily hereditary, often involving *germline mutations* in tumor suppressor genes (e.g., *BRCA1*, *BRCA2*).\n- *Gene-Environment Interaction*: Genetic *polymorphisms* in metabolic enzymes, such as the *Cytochrome P-450* system, influence how individuals process environmental procarcinogens.\n\n**Occupational Carcinogens**\n- *Asbestos*: Mesothelioma and lung carcinoma.\n- *Benzene*: Acute myeloid leukemia (AML).\n- *Vinyl Chloride*: Hepatic angiosarcoma.\n- *Arsenic*: Skin and lung carcinomas."
