          "generatedNotes": "**Molecular Basis of Carcinogenesis**\n\n- Carcinogenesis is a *multistep process* resulting from the accumulation of nonlethal genetic damage.\n- *Clonality*: Tumors originate from the *clonal expansion* of a single precursor cell harboring genetic alterations.\n- *Driver Mutations*: Mutations that contribute directly to the development of the malignant phenotype. The first is the *initiating mutation*.\n- *Passenger Mutations*: Neutral mutations that increase in frequency due to *genomic instability* but do not provide a growth advantage.\n- *Tumor Progression*: Through *Darwinian selection*, tumors evolve over time, leading to genetic *heterogeneity* as subclones compete for nutrients and niches.\n\n**Pathogenesis Pathway**\n\nNormal Cell &rarr; *Initiating Mutation* (Acquired/Inherited) &rarr; Accumulation of *Driver Mutations* &rarr; *Clonal Expansion* &rarr; *Genomic Instability* &rarr; Selective Pressure &rarr; *Malignant Neoplasm*\n\n**Oncogenes and Tumor Suppressor Genes**\n\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Feature</th>\n      <th>Proto-oncogenes / Oncogenes</th>\n      <th>Tumor Suppressor Genes</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Normal Function**</td>\n      <td>Promote cell growth and proliferation.</td>\n      <td>Inhibit cell growth or induce apoptosis.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Mutation Type**</td>\n      <td>*Gain-of-function* (Oncogene).</td>\n      <td>*Loss-of-function*.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Genetic Behavior**</td>\n      <td>Dominant (one allele mutated is sufficient).</td>\n      <td>Recessive (usually requires \"Two-Hits\").</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Example Mechanism**</td>\n      <td>Constitutive signaling/overexpression.</td>\n      <td>Release of cell cycle \"brakes.\"</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Key Oncogenes and Signal Transducers**\n\n- **RAS Family (KRAS, HRAS, NRAS)**: The most common proto-oncogene mutations in human cancers. Points mutations trap RAS in the *GTP-bound active state*, sending continuous growth signals.\n- **MYC**: A *master transcriptional regulator* that upregulates genes for cell cycle progression (*D cyclins*), rRNA assembly, and *metabolic reprogramming* (Warburg Effect).\n- **BCR-ABL**: A fusion gene created by *chromosomal translocation* t(9;22) (Philadelphia chromosome) in *Chronic Myeloid Leukemia*. Encodes a constitutively active *tyrosine kinase*.\n- **ERBB2 (HER2)**: Activated by *gene amplification* in breast carcinomas, leading to receptor overexpression and autonomous growth signaling.\n\n**Major Tumor Suppressor Genes**\n\n- **RB (Retinoblastoma Gene)**: The \"Governor\" of the cell cycle.\n  - *Active state*: Hypophosphorylated RB binds *E2F transcription factors*, blocking the G1 to S phase transition.\n  - *Inactive state*: Hyperphosphorylated by *CDK4/Cyclin D* complexes, releasing E2F to drive DNA replication.\n  - *Knudson’s Two-Hit Hypothesis*: Familial cases inherit one defective allele; sporadic cases require two acquired somatic mutations.\n- **TP53**: The \"Guardian of the Genome.\"\n  - Activated by *DNA damage* or hypoxia via *ATM/ATR* kinases.\n  - Functions: Induces *p21* (CDK inhibitor) for *quiescence* (temporary arrest), triggers *senescence* (permanent arrest), or initiates *apoptosis* (via *BAX* and *PUMA*) if damage is irreparable.\n- **APC (Adenomatous Polyposis Coli)**: Downregulates *WNT signaling*.\n  - Normal APC promotes a \"destruction complex\" that degrades *beta-catenin*.\n  - Loss of APC allows *beta-catenin* to translocate to the nucleus and activate growth-promoting genes like *MYC*.\n\n**Genomic Instability and DNA Repair**\n\n- **Mutator Phenotype**: Result of loss-of-function mutations in DNA repair genes, facilitating the rapid accumulation of driver mutations.\n- **Mismatch Repair (MSH2, MLH1)**: Defects lead to *microsatellite instability*, characteristic of *Hereditary Nonpolyposis Colon Cancer (Lynch Syndrome)*.\n- **Nucleotide Excision Repair**: Defects prevent repair of UV-induced *pyrimidine dimers*, causing *Xeroderma Pigmentosum*.\n- **Homologous Recombination (BRCA1, BRCA2)**: Essential for repairing double-strand DNA breaks. Mutations increase the risk of breast and ovarian carcinomas.\n\n**Epigenetic Alterations**\n\n- **DNA Hypermethylation**: Occurs at promoter regions to *silence tumor suppressor genes* (e.g., *CDKN2A*).\n- **Global Hypomethylation**: Can lead to genomic instability and inappropriate activation of oncogenes.\n- **Oncometabolites**: Mutated enzymes like *IDH (Isocitrate Dehydrogenase)* produce *2-hydroxyglutarate (2-HG)*, which inhibits DNA demethylating enzymes, causing oncogenic epigenetic shifts."
