          "topic": "Differentiate between carcinomas, sarcomas, and lymphoreticular neoplasms.",
          "generatedNotes": "**Classification of Malignant Neoplasms**\n\nThe primary classification of malignant tumors is based on their *parenchymal* component, which determines their biologic behavior and nomenclature.\n\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Feature</th>\n      <th>Carcinomas</th>\n      <th>Sarcomas</th>\n      <th>Lymphoreticular Neoplasms</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Tissue of Origin**</td>\n      <td>*Epithelial cell* origin (ectoderm, endoderm, or mesoderm).</td>\n      <td>Solid *mesenchymal* (connective) tissues.</td>\n      <td>*Blood-forming cells* or lymphoid tissues.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Common Subtypes**</td>\n      <td>*Adenocarcinoma* (glandular), *Squamous cell carcinoma* (stratified squamous).</td>\n      <td>*Fibrosarcoma*, *Chondrosarcoma*, *Osteosarcoma*, *Leiomyosarcoma*.</td>\n      <td>*Leukemias* (blood-based), *Lymphomas* (lymphocyte-based).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Physical Texture**</td>\n      <td>May be hard or *scirrhous* due to *desmoplasia* (abundant collagenous stroma).</td>\n      <td>Usually soft and *fleshy* (Greek *sar* = fleshy).</td>\n      <td>Often referred to as *liquid tumors*.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Primary Spread**</td>\n      <td>Prefer *lymphatic spread* (initial dissemination to regional nodes).</td>\n      <td>Prefer *hematogenous spread* (via veins to liver and lungs).</td>\n      <td>*Disseminated* at diagnosis; cells normally circulate in the bloodstream.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Carcinomas**\n\n- Derived from epithelial linings of glands, ducts, or surfaces.\n- *Adenocarcinoma*: Malignant epithelial cells growing in a *glandular pattern*.\n- *Squamous cell carcinoma*: Malignant cells resembling *stratified squamous epithelium*.\n- Spread Pathway: Usually follow natural routes of *lymphatic drainage* (e.g., breast carcinoma spreads to *axillary lymph nodes*).\n\n**Sarcomas**\n\n- Arise from *mesenchymal origin* including connective tissue, bone, muscle, and vessels.\n- Nomenclature: The suffix *-sarcoma* is attached to the cell type (e.g., *liposarcoma* from adipocytes).\n- Spread Pathway: *Hematogenous spread* is most typical. Because they often invade veins, the *liver* and *lungs* are the most frequent secondary sites.\n\n**Lymphoreticular Neoplasms**\n\n- These include *leukemias* and *lymphomas*.\n- Unlike most \"solid\" tumors, these are considered *malignant* at the time of diagnosis because they arise from cells that naturally have the capacity to enter the bloodstream.\n- *Lymphoma*: Specifically refers to tumors of *lymphocytes* or their precursors.\n\n**Diagnostic Nomenclature Exceptions**\n\nSeveral malignant neoplasms use the suffix *-oma*, which usually designates benign tumors, but these are strictly *malignant*:\n- *Lymphoma* (lymphoid tissue).\n- *Melanoma* (melanocytes).\n- *Mesothelioma* (mesothelium).\n- *Seminoma* (testicular epithelium).\n\n**The Pathway of Dissemination**\n\nMaligant cells &rarr; *Invasion* of local ECM/basement membrane &rarr; *Intravasation* into vessels/lymphatics &rarr; *Transport* to distant site &rarr; *Extravasation* &rarr; *Colonization* (Metastasis)."
