          "topic": "Explain the tumor grading and staging.",
          "generatedNotes": "**Tumor Grading**\n\n- *Definition*: A microscopic assessment used to quantify the probable clinical aggressiveness of a neoplasm.\n- *Criteria*: Based on the degree of *differentiation* (how closely tumor cells resemble normal parenchymal cells), the *number of mitoses*, and specific *architectural features*.\n- *Classification*: Typically categorized into two (low-grade vs. high-grade) or up to four levels of increasing anaplasia.\n- *Clinical Accuracy*: Correlation between histologic grade and actual biologic behavior is useful but not perfectly consistent.\n\n**Tumor Staging**\n\n- *Definition*: An assessment of the anatomic extent and spread of a cancer.\n- *Criteria*: Based on the size of the primary lesion, involvement of regional lymph nodes, and the presence or absence of distant metastases.\n- *TNM System*: The standardized classification system used by the *American Joint Committee on Cancer*.\n\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Component</th>\n      <th>Classification</th>\n      <th>Definition</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td><b>T (Primary Tumor)</b></td>\n      <td>T0</td>\n      <td>*In situ* lesion</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td></td>\n      <td>T1–T4</td>\n      <td>Increasing size and local extent</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><b>N (Regional Nodes)</b></td>\n      <td>N0</td>\n      <td>No nodal involvement</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td></td>\n      <td>N1–N3</td>\n      <td>Involvement of increasing number and range of nodes</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td><b>M (Metastases)</b></td>\n      <td>M0</td>\n      <td>No distant spread detected</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td></td>\n      <td>M1 (or M2)</td>\n      <td>Presence of distant blood-borne metastases</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Clinical Utility and Evolution**\n\n- *Prognostic Value*: Staging is considered of greater clinical importance than grading for predicting outcomes and determining treatment.\n- *Molecular Staging*: Modern protocols now incorporate *molecular characterization* (e.g., in early-stage breast cancer) alongside anatomic staging to provide independent prognostic data.\n\n**Diagnostic Sequence for Staging**\n\nHistologic Evaluation &rarr; Imaging/Surgical Exploration &rarr; TNM Scoring &rarr; Clinical Stage Assignment &rarr; Treatment Selection"
