          "topic": "Enlist common tumor markers with their clinical relevance.",
          "generatedNotes": "**Tumor Markers and Clinical Relevance**\n\nTumor markers are biochemical substances (enzymes, hormones, or proteins) found in the blood or body fluids that are associated with specific neoplasms. While generally lacking the *sensitivity* and *specificity* required for definitive primary diagnosis or population screening, they are vital for managing established disease.\n\n**Clinical Utility Guidelines**\n- **Treatment Response:** A successful surgical resection or therapeutic response typically results in the disappearance of the marker from the blood.\n- **Recurrence Monitoring:** The reappearance or persistence of a marker is a highly reliable indicator of *residual tumor* or *recurrent disease*.\n- **Personalized Therapy:** Identifying specific mutations (e.g., *EGFR* or *RAS*) helps select appropriate targeted drugs.\n\n**Common Tumor Markers**\n\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Category</th>\n      <th>Marker</th>\n      <th>Associated Neoplasms</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Hormones**</td>\n      <td>*Human Chorionic Gonadotropin (HCG)*</td>\n      <td>Trophoblastic tumors, nonseminomatous testicular tumors.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td></td>\n      <td>*Calcitonin*</td>\n      <td>Medullary carcinoma of the thyroid.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td></td>\n      <td>*Catecholamines* / Metabolites</td>\n      <td>Pheochromocytoma and related tumors.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Oncofetal Antigens**</td>\n      <td>*Alpha-Fetoprotein (AFP)*</td>\n      <td>Hepatocellular carcinoma, nonseminomatous germ cell tumors of testis, yolk sac tumors.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td></td>\n      <td>*Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA)*</td>\n      <td>Carcinomas of the colon, pancreas, lung, stomach, and breast.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Lineage-Specific Proteins**</td>\n      <td>*Prostate-Specific Antigen (PSA)*</td>\n      <td>Prostate cancer (also elevated in benign prostatic hyperplasia).</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td></td>\n      <td>*Immunoglobulins*</td>\n      <td>Multiple myeloma and other gammopathies.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Mucins / Glycoproteins**</td>\n      <td>*CA-125*</td>\n      <td>Ovarian cancer.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td></td>\n      <td>*CA-19-9*</td>\n      <td>Pancreatic and colon cancer.</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td></td>\n      <td>*CA-15-3*</td>\n      <td>Breast cancer.</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Molecular Markers and \"Liquid Biopsy\"**\n\nRecent advances allow for the detection of *cell-free DNA* or *circulating tumor DNA* shed into the blood or urine. These are used to identify specific *driver mutations* and monitor the emergence of *drug resistance*.\n\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Mutant DNA Marker</th>\n      <th>Target Cancer</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>*EGFR* mutants</td>\n      <td>Lung cancer</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>*TP53*, *APC*, *RAS* mutants</td>\n      <td>Colon cancer, pancreatic cancer</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>*TP53* mutants</td>\n      <td>Bladder cancer (in urine)</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Diagnostic and Surveillance Pathway**\n\nDetection of Elevated Marker &rarr; Tumor Diagnosis/Staging &rarr; Therapeutic Intervention (Surgery/Chemo) &rarr; Marker Level Decline (Baseline) &rarr; Periodic Monitoring &rarr; Marker Elevation &rarr; Identification of *Recurrence* or *Metastasis*."
