          "topic": "Describe the clinical features of common paraneoplastic syndromes.",
          "generatedNotes": "**Clinical Significance of Paraneoplastic Syndromes**\n\n- Approximately *10%* of patients with cancer experience these syndromes.\n- They frequently serve as the *initial sign* of an undetected malignancy.\n- Clinical severity can be life-threatening or significantly debilitating.\n- Syndromes often *simulate metastatic spread*, potentially leading to incorrect treatment strategies.\n\n**Major Endocrine Paraneoplastic Syndromes**\n\n- **Hypercalcemia**: Recognized as the *most frequent* paraneoplastic syndrome. It arises via two mechanisms: *osteolysis* (direct bone destruction by tumor) or *humoral hypercalcemia of malignancy* (secretion of systemic substances). Only the latter is strictly paraneoplastic.\n- **Cushing Syndrome**: Characterized by ectopic production of *ACTH* or its precursor, *pro-opiomelanocortin*. About 50% of cases are associated with *small-cell lung carcinoma*.\n- **Ectopic Hormone Secretion**: Tumors of non-endocrine origin synthesize hormones normally indigenous to other tissues.\n\n**Common Paraneoplastic Syndromes and Mechanisms**\n\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    <tr>\n      <th>Clinical Syndrome</th>\n      <th>Associated Malignancies</th>\n      <th>Causal Mechanism</th>\n    </tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Cushing Syndrome**</td>\n      <td>*Small-cell lung carcinoma*, pancreatic cancer</td>\n      <td>ACTH or ACTH-like peptides</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Hypercalcemia**</td>\n      <td>*Squamous cell lung carcinoma*, breast, renal cancer</td>\n      <td>*Parathyroid hormone-related protein (PTHRP)*, TGF-α</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Polycythemia**</td>\n      <td>*Renal cell carcinoma*, hepatocellular carcinoma</td>\n      <td>*Erythropoietin*</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Acanthosis Nigricans**</td>\n      <td>*Gastric carcinoma*, lung, uterine cancer</td>\n      <td>EGF secretion; Immunologic factors</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Trousseau Syndrome**</td>\n      <td>Pancreatic cancer, lung cancer</td>\n      <td>Hypercoagulability (mucins activating clotting)</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <td>**Hypertrophic Osteoarthropathy**</td>\n      <td>*Bronchogenic carcinoma*</td>\n      <td>Unknown; involves *digital clubbing*</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Neuromuscular and Dermatologic Features**\n\n- **Neuromyopathic Syndromes**: Presents as *peripheral neuropathy*, *cortical cerebellar degeneration*, or *myasthenic syndromes* (similar to myasthenia gravis).\n- **Mechanism**: An immune-mediated attack occurs when the immune system mistakenly targets normal nervous tissue that shares antigens with the tumor cells.\n- **Acanthosis Nigricans**: Thickened, gray-black, *velvety hyperkeratosis* of the skin. Often associated with the *Leser-Trélat sign* (sudden eruption of numerous seborrheic keratoses).\n\n**Vascular and Hematologic Manifestations**\n\n- **Migratory Thrombophlebitis**: Also called *Trousseau phenomenon*; recurring venous thromboses associated with deep-seated visceral cancers.\n- **Nonbacterial Thrombotic Endocarditis**: Formation of sterile, fibrinous vegetations on *cardiac valves* (typically left-sided), common in advanced mucin-secreting adenocarcinomas.\n- **Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC)**: Frequently complicates *acute promyelocytic leukemia* or *prostate cancer*.\n\n**High-Yield Pathway: Humoral Hypercalcemia**\n\n*Squamous Cell Lung Carcinoma* &rarr; Secretion of *PTHRP* (PTH-related protein) &rarr; Binding to *PTH receptors* &rarr; Increased *bone resorption* and *renal calcium retention* &rarr; *Hypercalcemia*"
